 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "s0",
   "title": "Two heats",
   "text": "Stroke \\(a\\to b\\) absorbs \\(Q_1=nRT_1\\ln\\dfrac{V_2}{V_1}\\) and stroke \\(c\\to d\\) rejects \\(Q_2=nRT_2\\ln\\dfrac{V_3}{V_4}\\). The graph above shows the same cycle on \\(T\\)–\\(S\\) axes (entropy \\(S\\) is **Beyond the book**): we read it at step 4.",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 6500
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "title": "The two logs are equal",
   "text": "From the previous derivation \\(V_3/V_4=V_2/V_1\\), so both heats contain the **same** logarithm \\(\\lambda=\\ln(V_2/V_1)\\): \\(Q_1=nRT_1\\lambda\\) and \\(Q_2=nRT_2\\lambda\\).",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 6000
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "title": "Divide",
   "text": "Divide \\(Q_2\\) by \\(Q_1\\). Everything cancels except the temperatures: \\(\\dfrac{Q_2}{Q_1}=\\dfrac{T_2}{T_1}\\). With \\(T_1={{T1|0}}\\) K and \\(T_2={{T2|0}}\\) K the ratio is \\({{rT|3}}\\).",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 6000
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "title": "Read the T–S picture",
   "text": "On a \\(T\\)–\\(S\\) graph (area under a line = heat, \\(Q=T\\,\\Delta S\\)) the Carnot cycle is a **rectangle**: the two adiabats are vertical because no heat means no change in \\(S\\). Both isotherms have the **same width** \\(\\Delta S\\), so their heats are in the ratio of their heights \\(T_2:T_1\\).",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 7000
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "title": "Entropy bookkeeping",
   "text": "The hot reservoir loses \\(Q_1/T_1\\) of entropy and the cold one gains \\(Q_2/T_2\\). The result above says these are **equal**, so the total change is zero. That is what \"reversible\" means.",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 6000
  },
  {
   "id": "s5",
   "title": "Numbers",
   "text": "For \\(V_2/V_1={{Vr|1}}\\) (1 mol): \\(Q_1={{Q1|0}}\\) J, \\(Q_2={{Q2|0}}\\) J, so \\(W=Q_1-Q_2={{Wn|0}}\\) J and \\(Q_2/Q_1={{rQ|3}}\\) — exactly \\(T_2/T_1\\).",
   "actions": [],
   "dwell": 6000
  }
 ],
